{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Moses assembled all the congregation of the children of Israel, etc.</b> Rashi explained: on the morrow of the Day of Atonement, etc. And in the portion of Jethro it is written (Exodus 18:13) ״וַיְהִי מִמָּחֳרָת וַיֵּשֶׁב מֹשֶׁה לִשְׁפֹּט אֶת הָעָם״, and Rashi explained there that this was on the morrow of the Day of Atonement. And it appears to me that it is well known that this assembly was to make known to them the commandment of the Tabernacle and the donation, as will be explained shortly. And Moses was concerned lest one of them donate to the Tabernacle something that is not his, while he thinks that he holds it lawfully; and this is impossible for building this great and holy House from stolen goods — 'the place of justice, there the wickedness' (Ecclesiastes 3:16). Therefore Moses first proclaimed: whoever has a lawsuit, let him approach me for judgment, so that all the people may come to their place in peace, and each person would know what is his and what is not his through his judging between them. And only then would he make known to them the matter of the donation, saying ״קְחוּ מֵאִתְּכֶם תְּרוּמָה לַה׳״ — and ״מֵאִתְּכֶם״ means from your own property and not from your fellow's, for otherwise ״מֵאִתְּכֶם״ is superfluous. And even though without doubt he could not judge all the people in a single day, and even on that day he did not sit in judgment the whole day — for even though it is stated (Exodus 18:13) 'from morning until evening,' that is, according to Rashi's explanation, because whoever judges a true judgment truly, Scripture accounts it as though he became a partner to the Holy One, blessed be He, in the work of Creation, as it says (Genesis 1:5) ״וַיְהִי עֶרֶב וַיְהִי בֹקֶר״ — if not so, when did he make known to them the work of the Tabernacle? In any case, the donation too was not completed in one day. And perhaps he informed them that anything about which two parties are in dispute — they should not give it as a donation until the judgment is first clarified with whom it belongs." "And by way of allusion we may say that this assembly was to forge peace among them, for a person does not dwell with a snake in a single basket. And once he wanted to make known to them the work of the Tabernacle, in which they would all be partners, it is as though he seated them all in a single dwelling. Therefore he needed to assemble them first, so that they would be in a single union; and therefore Rashi explained that this was on the morrow of the Day of Atonement, because all the encampments were in strife and murmuring except for the encampment before the giving of the Torah, as it says (Exodus 19:2) ״וַיִּחַן שָׁם יִשְׂרָאֵל״ etc. And if so, how is it possible to assemble them at a time when they are divided and their minds are not of one accord, and this is not a fitting time? Therefore Moses found an opening to assemble them as he wished on the morrow of the Day of Atonement, for on the Day of Atonement peace intercedes between them, and on that very day they are all in a single union. Therefore it was easy to assemble them on the morrow, as long as yesterday's peace still held. But if a day or two were to pass, then the peace would not endure, for the company had already dispersed and each man turns to his own way. And lest the peace be opposed by words of strife between them in monetary matters between a man and his fellow — for the peace of the Day of Atonement has no dealings with this — therefore Moses also sat to judge on that very day, so that from every side there would be peace among them. And then they would be fit to dwell in a single dwelling, namely the Tabernacle shared by all of them. And once they became unified through the Tabernacle that fused them together — from then on we find many times that Moses assembled them even though it was not the morrow of the Day of Atonement. And all the more so concerning what is written in the Akeidah in an allegorical manner on the verse ״לֹא תְבַעֲרוּ אֵשׁ״ etc. — that they should not kindle the fire of strife on the day of Shabbat, when they are idle from labor, and one must be all the more concerned then about the fire of strife that catches among idle talk. If so, he said well ״וַיַּקְהֵל״ — he assembled them to be in a single union through the commandment of ״לֹא תְבַעֲרוּ אֵשׁ״, for at his word they gather." "<b>These are the things which HaShem commanded to do them.</b> ״דְּבָרִים״ — plural in meaning, yet He did not tell them at that time anything but the commandment of Shabbat. For one cannot say that ״דְּבָרִים״ refers also to the Tabernacle, which he mentioned afterward, for behold it says ״זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳״ — if so, with ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ he spoke of the Tabernacle, and what do I fulfill with ״אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים״? And further it is difficult that he said ״שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים תֵּעָשֶׂה מְלָאכָה״ — he should have said ״תַּעֲשֶׂה״, for ״תֵּעָשֶׂה״ means 'on its own.' And further it is difficult that this entire verse is superfluous. And what is written in the Decalogue, ״שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים תַּעֲבֹד״ — we already explained this in the portion of Jethro; see there. But this verse does not tolerate that explanation." "Therefore I say that he used ״דְּבָרִים״ because the main essence of this commandment was to command concerning the work of the Tabernacle — that they should engage in it for all six days of labor, and on the seventh day they should not engage in it, for the Tabernacle does not override Shabbat. And there were here two things: one, to command concerning the making of the Tabernacle; the second, that they should not engage in it on Shabbat. And what is said, ״תֵּעָשֶׂה מְלָאכָה״, is because the main essence of this command was concerning the bringing of the donation, for this too is called מְלָאכָה, as our Sages of blessed memory taught (Shabbat 96b) from the verse ״אִישׁ וְאִשָּׁה אַל יַעֲשׂוּ עוֹד מְלָאכָה״, and it is written ״וַיִּכָּלֵא הָעָם מֵהָבִיא״. If so, bringing the donation from one domain to another is called מְלָאכָה. And concerning that מְלָאכָה he said ״תֵּעָשֶׂה״, for he could not say ״תַּעֲשֶׂה מְלָאכָה״ by way of command, since the thing given by way of donation is not a command and obligation, but rather ״כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר נְדָבוֹ לִבּוֹ״ gives of himself. Therefore it says ״תֵּעָשֶׂה מְלָאכָה״ — 'on its own' it means. ״וּבַיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי שַׁבָּת לַה׳״ — and the bringing of the donation from one domain to another is also forbidden on it. And according to the midrash of our Sages of blessed memory, which says that the Tabernacle was made partly on its own (see Exodus Rabbah 52:4) — such as the Menorah, which was made on its own, and likewise the erection of the Tabernacle was on its own, as it says (Exodus 40:17) ״הוּקַם הַמִּשְׁכָּן״ — therefore it says ״תֵּעָשֶׂה מְלָאכָה״: 'on its own' it means."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:25:39.516996541Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}